00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:14.156
FRANCESC CAMPOY: One
of the favorite topics.

00:02:14.156 --> 00:02:15.780
MARK MANDEL: One of
my favorite topics.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.510
So there is an article that
came out on the Kubernetes blog

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.660
about running highly
available Kubernetes clusters.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.020
So basically, the standard
Kubernetes system right now

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.210
runs on a single master.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.330
That works reasonably well in
that if the master falls over

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.390
and-- it's fine.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.931
It doesn't actually
destroy anything.

00:02:32.931 --> 00:02:35.760
But if people have been looking
for multi-master setups,

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.510
especially high-availability
setups across multiple regions,

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.050
there's a great article
showing how that's currently

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.567
been supported on Google Compute
Engine in the Kubernetes 1.5

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:48.150
release, and shows you all the
commands you need to use to set

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.225
that up, which is pretty cool.

00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:50.141
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:02:50.141 --> 00:02:55.020
So what happens now if
you're saying that if--

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.735
now we have two masters
for higher availability?

00:02:57.735 --> 00:02:59.860
What happens if you have
a master and it goes down?

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.160
Does that mean that the
whole cluster goes down?

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.650
MARK MANDEL: No, it doesn't,
which is pretty good.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.040
So you should
monitor your masters.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:05.748
That's important.

00:03:05.748 --> 00:03:08.022
But if your master does
go down, all your pods

00:03:08.022 --> 00:03:10.230
continue to keep running
like they would do normally.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.180
So this is how you
can do upgrades.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.834
And if something horrible
happens, then that's great.

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:15.750
Then that's not great.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.940
That's-- something
horrible happens.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.417
Then you're covered.

00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:21.000
Your services aren't
going to go down.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.710
And your systems aren't
going to go down.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.834
But if your master goes
down, keep an eye on it

00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:26.250
and then bring it
back up quickly.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.200
But it's not going to mean
your services are going to die,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.340
which is pretty cool.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.440
Following on the
topic of Kubernetes,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.430
there is another blog post--

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:38.880
