00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:24.600
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.490
It looks like we have
pre-configured images

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.350
for Microsoft SQL
Server Enterprise

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.110
and Windows Server
Core on Compute Engine.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.026
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:04:31.026 --> 00:04:33.090
And I think that one of
the coolest parts of this

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.470
is there's also some
instructions to set them up

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.105
with high availability and
disaster recovery, which

00:04:41.105 --> 00:04:42.480
means that basically
you're going

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.360
to be running every single
instance in a different zone.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.870
So even if one whole
zone goes down,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.790
you will still
have your database

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.010
running all the time,
which is very important--

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.176
and disaster recovery, which
means that you will not

00:04:55.176 --> 00:04:57.900
lose a single piece of data,
which is something that we all

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:58.442
want to have.

00:04:58.442 --> 00:05:00.399
MARK MANDEL: I think this
is also kind of cool.

00:05:00.399 --> 00:05:03.030
It's mentioned here, as well,
that finally all of the Windows

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.760
Server images are now enabled
with Windows Remote Management

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.120
Support, which is pretty nice
if you need to manage fleets

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.184
