00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:03.084
So yeah-- a little
bit of everything.

00:07:03.084 --> 00:07:04.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.920
So I'm actually curious about--

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.150
you told the story a
little bit of how you got

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:10.830
started with Kubernetes.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.640
But how did fission.io start?

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:16.890
When did someone be
like, oh, we should

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.800
have functions on Kubernetes?

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.550
Could you tell us a
little bit about that?

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:21.300
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.500
Yeah.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.170
So the actual thing
started in a hackathon.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.440
We have these little
internal company hackathons

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:29.910
every few months.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:36.370
And I'd been simultaneously
excited about AWS Lambda

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:40.080
and serverless
around the same time,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.240
and also because we
were taking Kubernetes

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:48.690
to companies who hadn't really
worked with containers before

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:49.770
at all.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.770
And so both Docker and
Kubernetes were new to them.

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:54.900
They knew that they were
excited about Kubernetes

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.599
and wanted to use it.

00:07:56.599 --> 00:07:58.140
And we take it to
them and deploy it.

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.210
And then they would--

00:07:59.210 --> 00:08:01.140
their next question
would be, how do I

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:02.670
do something useful with this?

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:07.230
And we didn't have any
helpful answer other than,

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.100
go read the manual, try out
some examples, and so on.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.180
So we really wanted something
where we can give users

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.520
something where-- a simple,
a really simple app--

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.430
can be brought up
in the first day

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.020
that you have a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:25.890
And so this hackathon
provided a really--

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.660
a short way to make
some kind of prototype.

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:33.419
That's when we made this
Node.js thing, which basically

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:38.039
wrapped a Node.js function
at runtime into a container

00:08:38.039 --> 00:08:40.049
and brought it up as a
service, and you could

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:42.030
start doing requests to it.

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:45.390
So it is early prototype
about six months ago.

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:46.140
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.546
So on the Fission website,
it says serverless functions

00:08:49.546 --> 00:08:50.170
for Kubernetes.

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:50.570
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.170
MARK MANDEL: What
does that mean?

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:53.970
What does that actually
look like, say,

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:58.010
as a developer experience,
and also just conceptually?

00:08:58.010 --> 00:09:00.350
SOAM VASANI: So even though
we say functions is actually

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:04.170
modules more properly,
so you write a--

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:06.430
so the programmer writes
a Node.js or Python

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:09.990
module with one function
as the entry point,

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:12.030
as the sole export
of that module.

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.070
And then Fission takes
that and turns it

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:17.910
into an HTTP service
that runs on Kubernetes.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.640
And it also manages the
lifecycle of that service,

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:23.900
creating it, instantiating
it when there's a request,

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.140
killing it when it's
idle, scaling it,

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.360
potentially-- things like that.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:31.380
So the programmer has
a simple interface.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:35.940
They write a module and a
function which takes a context.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:39.210
The context contains
the request, and so on.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:41.160
And they just implement
the service there.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.830
And then Fission is
responsible for taking it

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:47.654
from a source code file to a
running service on Kubernetes.

00:09:47.654 --> 00:09:48.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.960
So you mentioned one
thing, which I guess

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.650
is because of the way you
build your containers, which

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.470
is you said that you were
able to do Python and Node.js.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:03.390
I am myself a little
bit of a gopher.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:08.130
Is Go coming at some point
or is there any reasons why

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.030
Go is not supported?

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:11.113
And you say you're supporting
these languages that

00:10:11.113 --> 00:10:13.490
are interpreted, which
I assume there is?

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:14.240
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:14.810
Yeah.

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:16.580
So this is an exciting topic.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.590
This is what we're
working on right now.

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:20.130
MARK MANDEL: Yay.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.160
SOAM VASANI: So
today, our function--

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.430
it goes from a source
code file directly

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:27.260
into the execution environment.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:30.860
And Fission framework
hits a dynamic loader

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:34.550
that's inside that container and
loads that function and routes

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:35.540
requests to it.

00:10:35.540 --> 00:10:38.720
So now we want to enhance
this pipeline a bit

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:40.640
to add support for
compiled languages,

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:45.290
to add support for
dependency resolution step--

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:49.550
so installing
dependencies, linking them

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:51.960
in whatever way that's
defined in the language.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.740
So what we're doing
is taking a step back,

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.980
looking at how we define
these language environment,

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:01.280
and think, today we just have
an execution environment.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:04.040
Now you also need a
build environment.

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.270
And the build environment is--

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.690
Fission will just
say, your source code

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:13.160
goes as some sort of artifact
into the build environment

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.830
and in a language specific where
the built environment turns

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:20.300
that source artifact
into a dynamically

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:24.200
loadable, runnable artifact.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:29.260
So in the case of Go, you would
upload a set of source files

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:32.670
and maybe some spec
of dependencies.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:36.110
And then the builder would
output either a binary

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:40.610
or maybe plugins using
Go's newish plugin support.

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:42.140
And then the
execution environment

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.340
would load that plugin.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:48.230
And Fission would be responsible
for orchestrating this, running

00:11:48.230 --> 00:11:52.850
the build when the user uploads
a function, managing compile

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:56.270
errors and that
iteration cycle, and then

00:11:56.270 --> 00:12:00.525
transporting the binary, storing
it, caching it, and so on.

00:12:02.966 --> 00:12:04.340
The infrastructure
for Go support

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.590
is being built right now.

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:08.965
And then on top of that, we'll
build the Go lang support.

00:12:08.965 --> 00:12:11.298
MARK MANDEL: Do you have other
languages in the pipeline

00:12:11.298 --> 00:12:12.000
as well?

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:12.750
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:12:12.750 --> 00:12:15.360
So we already have
Node.js, Python.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:21.460
Somebody contributed
PHP and C# .NET.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:23.480
The C# .NET support
is interesting

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.360
because it's a
compiled language.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:29.156
But what it does is it
compiles it at runtime.

00:12:29.156 --> 00:12:34.550
So that's an interesting
way to do it.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:35.850
It's not ideal.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.700
We want to change that.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.660
But it is supported for now.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:41.160
So yeah.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.560
Those are the languages.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:46.732
There's a contributor who's
working on Ruby right now.

00:12:46.732 --> 00:12:48.440
Like I said, we're
working on the support

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:49.790
for compiled languages.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:50.690
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:52.610
So you touched on it
a little bit before,

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:55.910
but I'd like to talk
about it specifically.

00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:57.200
Why serverless?

00:12:57.200 --> 00:12:58.896
Why is that a thing?

00:12:58.896 --> 00:13:00.020
What does that mean to you?

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.850
And why was this
so important to you

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:05.559
when you came up
with this project?

00:13:05.559 --> 00:13:07.100
SOAM VASANI: So the
term "serverless"

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:08.560
itself is a bit vague.

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.990
So I think the general
idea is that it's

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:20.300
to sort of ask, what's the
simplest interface you can have

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:25.340
to write applications that
still can be used in production,

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:29.570
that's still useful, usable to
create services in production?

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:31.630
And I think this
Functions as a Service

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:34.220
is one approach to that.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:36.160
I'd say it's not
100% proven yet.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:39.430
But we think it'll get there.

00:13:42.541 --> 00:13:43.915
So I think Functions
as a Service

00:13:43.915 --> 00:13:52.100
is sort of maybe a more
precise or a more specific

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.777
form of serverless.

00:13:53.777 --> 00:13:55.610
But to answer your
question, why serverless?

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:59.090
I think it's-- there's a
pattern there of saying,

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:04.625
how simple can we make
this and still be useful?

00:14:04.625 --> 00:14:06.500
I think there's different
approaches to that.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:08.630
And Functions as a
Service is one approach

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:09.729
that I am excited about.

00:14:09.729 --> 00:14:12.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
question that I have now-- it's

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.580
the question that
pretty much started

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:19.550
this episode, which was not that
long ago, we had the monolith.

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.200
And we had one binary.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.810
And for that binary,
we had a bunch of code.

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.070
That's it.

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:28.280
Now, next, we move
to microservices.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.900
And all of a sudden, we have
a bunch of different binaries

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:31.880
or processes.

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.460
And at the same
time, we might have

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.830
a bunch of different repos.

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:39.800
And then now we're
moving to functions.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:43.340
And I'm curious about how do you
manage the complexity of having

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:47.810
so many different
pieces of source code

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:52.430
that are somehow related
but possibly stored

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:53.450
in different places?

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:54.980
How do you manage them?

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:56.855
SOAM VASANI: So that's
a few questions there.

00:14:56.855 --> 00:14:59.990
I think when you have a
large set of functions,

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:02.120
how do you put them together?

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:05.500
We decompose data application
into a bunch of little pieces.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:08.810
Now how do we put the
pieces back together?

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:13.750
And compatibility is
one of the issues there.

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:17.770
So I think this is a bit of
an open area for Functions

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:19.120
as a Service.

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:24.560
I think it's a bit of a language
and framework design problem.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.690
So we've got to
try a few things.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.170
So I think one
thing that we need

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:32.830
is support for some
sort of composability.

00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:35.710
So you need using code
to be able to compose

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:38.540
other functions together.

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:44.470
Today, most of the
FaaS implementations

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:46.530
don't do that very well.

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:48.460
You create an event
or HTTP request.

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:50.350
And you can call
other functions.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:53.770
But there isn't a formal
way to call other functions

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:56.080
through the framework itself.

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:57.730
And so we need that.

00:15:57.730 --> 00:15:59.890
So we need some sort
of composability.

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:02.290
And I think we need
some sort of grouping,

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:06.280
either modules, namespaces,
packages, something

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:09.010
like that that can
let you assemble

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:10.850
your functions together.

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:14.860
So you still have some notion
of separation between functions.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:17.440
But a program, after
all, like you said,

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.700
is many functions and modules.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:24.910
So you need some
constructs to group them.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:28.750
And then that will also help
with the compatibility question

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:32.600
because then you can version
the repository as a whole.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:34.900
So we don't have
any of this today.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:39.490
But it's some ideas
on what to do.

00:16:39.490 --> 00:16:41.410
For example, you
might have a Git repo

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:46.210
with a manifest at the top
level, something that looks

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:48.430
kind of like a
Kubernetes YAML file.

00:16:48.430 --> 00:16:55.000
And so it would say, this
subdirectory or this set

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:56.830
of files over here is--

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:58.650
forms a function here.

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.960
It imports that
module over there.

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:06.950
And this other set of files
is this other function.

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:10.310
And the whole thing is
versioned as a whole.

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:13.869
And so you could
imagine deploying.

00:17:13.869 --> 00:17:17.940
You could imagine pointing the
Fission tools at that manifest,

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:21.210
and the thing wraps it up
as some sort of package.

00:17:21.210 --> 00:17:24.420
And Fission figures
out how to run that.

00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:27.040
So that's very hand-wavy
and a little vague.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:32.020
But we want to experiment
with something like that.

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:35.440
Like I said, it's
not a solved problem.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:41.040
And we've got to try things
out and see how that goes.

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:43.720
Today, the main
utility tends to be,

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.420
maybe because of this
problem, in smaller use

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:50.920
cases, especially event handling
and webhooks, a small slack

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:52.456
bar, that sort of thing.

00:17:52.456 --> 00:17:53.830
MARK MANDEL: That's
actually what

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:55.955
I was going to actually
segue into, which is great.

00:17:55.955 --> 00:17:58.780
I was going to ask you,
then, given what you said,

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:01.859
what do you think the
major use cases for this?

00:18:01.859 --> 00:18:04.150
I know personally, like when
I looked at this, the fact

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.760
that phishing can handle
not only HTTP events,

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:09.010
but also Kubernetes events--

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:11.290
to do the glue stuff
within Kubernetes.

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:13.345
Was that what you think
it would be used for,

00:18:13.345 --> 00:18:15.400
or what are the use
cases you think are going

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:16.900
to be particularly useful here?

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:18.580
SOAM VASANI: So I
think initially,

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:23.860
it's very useful for small
REST APIs and maybe--

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.080
a webhook is basically
a one-endpoint REST API,

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:27.880
you could say--

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:31.840
and slack bars and
little automation,

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:39.310
like OK, let's pipe events from
Kubernetes or other sources

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:42.880
into that system and
use that to glue it

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:45.580
into other infrastructure.

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:49.030
I expect that to be
the main use case.

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:54.350
And I see the actual usage
also tends to be like that.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:19:00.700
So we haven't [AUDIO OUT] some
CI systems or automated builds,

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:02.350
triggers, some functions.

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:06.430
And we track, test failures,
and so on using these things.

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:11.610
The idea of having a
bigger app implemented

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:14.740
this way-- it's definitely
something we'd like to reach.

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:19.360
But that's definitely a
more speculative use case.

00:19:19.360 --> 00:19:21.030
We'll have to see how that goes.

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:22.730
I'm excited about that use case.

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:26.800
I think it would be really
nice to be able to specify,

00:19:26.800 --> 00:19:31.690
mostly in a language-neutral
way, how a REST API should

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:37.480
be deployed and run on
Fission, and have Fission take

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:40.090
care of that after
deployment and scaling

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:41.500
and things like that.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:44.772
But we're not quite there
yet for bigger programs.

00:19:44.772 --> 00:19:46.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
I'm actually curious.

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:49.490
You mentioned a
couple of use cases,

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:51.730
like managing some
events and stuff.

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:53.950
Could you tell us a
little bit about what

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:59.380
are the use cases for, which
you think that Functions

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:03.670
as a Service is a much better
metaphor than, let's say,

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:07.380
Infrastructure as a Service
or Platform as a Service?

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.810
SOAM VASANI: Again, like
today, the event handling stuff

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:17.580
is the best use case for that.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:22.230
If you just want to write,
say, some trigger for--

00:20:22.230 --> 00:20:24.830
for example, we have--

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:27.020
we were just discussing
this within the company

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:28.620
a few days ago--

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:31.410
we have this source code
cross-referencing tool

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:35.190
that today polls our
Git repositories.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:37.350
And so we figured
we could just--

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:41.550
we could change it to have
a small webhook that's

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:44.760
triggered every time you
do a commit to a repo.

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:47.850
And that webhooks would go and
poke this source code indexing

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.280
thing that would re-index
whatever changed,

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.950
or at least the
repo that changed.

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:56.964
And then you wouldn't
have to pull the Git repo.

00:20:56.964 --> 00:20:58.380
So now if you
imagine implementing

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:02.040
this as either Infrastructure
as a Service or microservices,

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.450
you have to--

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:06.540
it's a little bit more involved.

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:08.310
You have to create
the service around it.

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:09.990
If you're running
it on the VM, you

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:13.320
have to worry about the
whole operating system.

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:17.420
If you're on Kubernetes, you're
definitely a bit better off.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:20.040
But I think where this
Function as a Service helps

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:23.700
is that you don't
quite have to worry

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:27.420
as much about the capacity
of the cluster in terms

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:29.310
of the number of
services you deploy.

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.650
So in some sense,
creating a service

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:36.610
becomes cheaper, both in
terms of development cost

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:41.580
and runtime infrastructure cost.

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:43.830
So basically, our
cluster capacity

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:50.160
becomes more a function of usage
than of the size and number

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:51.780
of services that
you've deployed.

00:21:51.780 --> 00:21:52.530
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:55.080
So I'm curious about
something else as well.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:57.420
Fission is built on
top of Kubernetes.

00:21:57.420 --> 00:21:58.170
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.628
MARK MANDEL: Why did you choose
Kubernetes as the platform?

00:22:00.628 --> 00:22:03.507
What was the
reasoning behind that?

00:22:03.507 --> 00:22:05.340
SOAM VASANI: So a few
different things-- one

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:10.680
is that Kubernetes provides
so much of the stuff

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:13.960
that we'd have to build if
we didn't use Kubernetes

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:16.890
or something like it that, from
a purely implementation point

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:19.860
of view, it just
makes a lot of sense.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.690
We don't even know about nodes.

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:24.840
Kubernetes completely takes
care of the cluster management

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:25.950
itself.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:28.200
We don't have any
real scheduler.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.850
We might have some
scheduling decisions to make.

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:35.100
But Kubernetes takes care of the
bulk of scheduling decisions.

00:22:35.100 --> 00:22:39.900
We don't have to do any
complex storage integration.

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:42.120
We would just be able
to use Kubernetes

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:47.340
volumes, its networking,
secret management.

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:49.560
So in terms of
implementations, Kubernetes

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:54.510
takes us way ahead than we
would if we started without it.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.330
So that's one story from an
implementation point of view.

00:22:57.330 --> 00:23:01.210
From a user's point
of view, we think--

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:06.110
so I don't think Functions as
a Service will replace the idea

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:11.340
of microservices or just
putting services in containers

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:16.230
and running long-lived services
because there's plenty of use

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:19.812
cases where you want
to do that for--

00:23:19.812 --> 00:23:22.530
like let's say
you're running Redis.

00:23:22.530 --> 00:23:25.620
It doesn't make any
sense to kill Redis

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:27.450
when it isn't doing
requests because it

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:29.250
would lose all its memory.

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:32.820
So obviously, that has to
be a long-lived process.

00:23:32.820 --> 00:23:36.240
So if you're using
that, then the question

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.130
is, as part of your bigger--

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:42.240
as part of your
overall solution,

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:45.780
how do you mix together
Functions as a Service

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:47.760
and regular services?

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.550
And I think Kubernetes
is a good place

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:53.220
to bring those all
together because you

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:59.150
can get service discovery,
maybe, between these things.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:03.000
You can just easily have
them on the same cluster.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.820
You could in the future
maybe do some smart things

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:06.960
with scheduling.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:08.170
And what else?

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:12.090
So it's basically
[? interrupt ?] with services,

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:16.020
the implementation
advantages, the reuse

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:21.330
of Kubernetes concepts, like
secrets, volumes, and so on.

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:25.020
And I think there's also
an operational benefit.

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:28.170
You don't want to run
two clusters for--

00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:30.570
one for services and
one for functions.

00:24:30.570 --> 00:24:34.150
It's better to have fewer
systems that you manage.

00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:37.140
So if you're already managing
a Kubernetes cluster,

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:40.140
managing its nodes,
managing etcd and its state,

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:44.640
then you might as
well spend that--

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:48.240
you might as well reuse that
effort to run functions on.

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:51.270
And so if you have something
like a log monitoring framework

00:24:51.270 --> 00:24:53.490
and monitoring stuff
like Prometheus

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:56.444
you could just reuse
that with Fission.

00:24:56.444 --> 00:24:57.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:24:59.230
So I'm curious about--

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:01.290
you were talking about
all the cool things

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:03.120
about building on Kubernetes.

00:25:03.120 --> 00:25:05.690
So I'm going to go the
other way and ask you

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:10.010
what was the biggest challenge
about building something

00:25:10.010 --> 00:25:12.320
like Fission on
top of Kubernetes?

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:16.460
SOAM VASANI: For the most part,
once I got past the learning

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:20.480
curve, figured out how to use
the client, a lot of things

00:25:20.480 --> 00:25:22.590
were super doable.

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:25.520
The API is very complete.

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:27.244
It's very comprehensive.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:33.390
I could talk about a few
challenges that we just

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.740
haven't solved yet.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:38.730
So one thing is volumes.

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:41.870
I spoke about how it's
awesome that we can reuse

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:43.920
Kubernetes' storage management.

00:25:43.920 --> 00:25:46.290
But the way volumes
work conflicts

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:50.710
with the way we
dynamically load functions.

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:52.260
So let me roll back a bit.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:56.550
When we started this, we
tried to just take a function,

00:25:56.550 --> 00:26:00.600
wrap it in an
image, and just do--

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:02.340
just create a
Kubernetes deployment

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:06.120
on a service using
that image on demand.

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:08.850
But that takes a
few seconds at best.

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:13.140
Even if you pre-pull the
image and cache it everywhere,

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:16.080
that still takes a few seconds.

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:18.420
And when people do
Functions as a Service,

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:22.680
they expect a faster
cold start on the order

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:26.560
of 100 milliseconds,
even less sometimes.

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:29.730
But that's the goal
we've aimed for so far.

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:35.550
So to do that, we needed to
have already running ports.

00:26:35.550 --> 00:26:37.720
And so when you create a--

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:43.590
when you add support for
Node.js or Python to Fission,

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:48.450
then Fission creates a pool
of these pods that are already

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:52.242
running before you even have
any requests for a function.

00:26:52.242 --> 00:26:53.700
And then when the
request comes in,

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:56.730
the function's loaded
in there and run.

00:26:56.730 --> 00:27:00.390
And that has a smaller
overhead because the overhead

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:03.150
of starting a pod
was already taken

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:06.690
before the request came in.

00:27:06.690 --> 00:27:10.020
So now what to do with
things like volumes,

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:14.640
because it's not possible
today, neither in Docker not

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:19.050
Kubernetes, to mount a volume
on an existing running pod,

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:19.900
as far as I know.

00:27:19.900 --> 00:27:21.660
I have tried to figure this out.

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:26.020
But it looks like that
is not possible today.

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:29.250
So if your function
wants to use a volume,

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:33.870
then you have to lose the
cold start optimization, which

00:27:33.870 --> 00:27:37.620
means you have to start the pod
when the function request comes

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:45.630
in or we have to figure out
something separate from the way

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:50.760
volumes work today, like maybe
have a separate pod that then

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:54.450
the function can connect
to to get at its storage--

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:56.420
something like that.

00:27:56.420 --> 00:28:00.690
But basically, mounting
storage is a challenge

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:03.060
we haven't figured out yet.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:04.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Maybe
one of our listeners

00:28:04.890 --> 00:28:05.940
may be able to help with that.

00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:06.180
Who knows?

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:06.930
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:08.430
I hope so.

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:09.230
Yeah.

00:28:09.230 --> 00:28:10.190
Yeah.

00:28:10.190 --> 00:28:12.900
Today, the answer
is that we would

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:18.380
have maybe some sort of
separate mode where you just--

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.220
where you lose the
cold start optimization

00:28:20.220 --> 00:28:21.434
if you really want volumes.

00:28:21.434 --> 00:28:22.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:25.296
So I have one more
very short question.

00:28:25.296 --> 00:28:27.170
What language did you
used to implement this?

00:28:27.170 --> 00:28:28.250
SOAM VASANI: It's all in Go.

00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:29.000
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:30.370
SOAM VASANI: Yeah.

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:33.490
It's actually my first
non-trivial Go project--

00:28:33.490 --> 00:28:38.270
so very much learning as I go.

00:28:38.270 --> 00:28:44.910
I had a few initial
puzzling moments with Go.

00:28:44.910 --> 00:28:45.950
But now I like it a lot.

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:48.450
And I'm very excited
to be using it.

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:50.750
I sort of started with
it because Kubernetes

00:28:50.750 --> 00:28:51.910
was written in it.

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:54.600
And also, it seemed
to be getting popular.

00:28:54.600 --> 00:28:58.460
I didn't have a strong reason
to adopt one over the--

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:01.680
Go over something like
Node.js, which is what

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:03.360
I was familiar with before.

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:06.960
But I've really enjoyed
making this in Go.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:07.870
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:08.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.173
MARK MANDEL: We're definitely
running out of time.

00:29:10.173 --> 00:29:13.740
So very quickly, what is
the plans for the future?

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:15.895
What's in the
roadmap for Fission?

00:29:15.895 --> 00:29:18.330
SOAM VASANI: So I
think we addressed

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:23.130
a few major pieces of effort.

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:25.950
The current major effort
is improving that function

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.440
pipeline, supporting
compiled languages.

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:31.890
After that, we want to get
at improving the developer

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:34.860
workflow a bit, dealing with--

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:37.500
having better support
for versioning.

00:29:37.500 --> 00:29:39.960
We have very, very basic
versioning support today.

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:44.760
There is support, but
it's a bit manual--

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:49.620
doesn't [? interrupt ?] with
Git as nicely as we like.

00:29:49.620 --> 00:29:53.400
And then there's more language
support, better integration

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:56.610
with Kubernetes'
volume, secrets,

00:29:56.610 --> 00:30:01.350
config maps, and of course,
integrations with more event

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:04.590
sources, integration with
some sort of message queue.

00:30:04.590 --> 00:30:10.010
There's NATS-- maybe Kafka
or something like that.

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:13.770
And finally, we have a lot
of performance tuning to do.

00:30:13.770 --> 00:30:15.180
We don't do auto-scaling today.

00:30:15.180 --> 00:30:18.450
But we need to do
that both up and down,

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:22.930
things like that, and
also latency improvements,

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:25.170
both in cold start
and in the fast path.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:26.820
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:30:26.820 --> 00:30:28.707
Soam, thank you so much
