00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:28.360
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:30.390
Well, why don't we move into
our Question of the Week?

00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:31.740
I know you had a
particularly interesting one

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:32.700
about WebSockets.

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:33.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:35.310
So as I was saying at the
beginning of the episode,

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:38.850
it's about-- it's something that
I really don't know that much,

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:40.870
but it is important.

00:31:40.870 --> 00:31:44.460
Many people know that, if
you have a global service,

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:46.530
you need to do load balancing.

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:48.150
And many people
know that, if you

00:31:48.150 --> 00:31:50.130
want to have a very
responsive web page,

00:31:50.130 --> 00:31:52.020
WebSockets can help you.

00:31:52.020 --> 00:31:54.480
And we all know that it
is important to secure

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:57.180
all of the things with HTTPS.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:02.460
But when you try to use
WebSockets over HTTPS load

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:05.100
balancer, if you read the
documentation, it says, no,

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:06.000
you cannot do that.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:07.943
What's the deal with this?

00:32:07.943 --> 00:32:09.372
How do you do it?

00:32:09.372 --> 00:32:10.830
MARK MANDEL: What
is going on here?

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:11.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
What is going on?

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:13.139
MARK MANDEL: What is going on?

00:32:13.139 --> 00:32:15.270
So it's a good question,
and one I've definitely

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:16.240
run into before.

00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:19.600
So yes, if you're doing HTTP
or HTTPS load balancing,

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:22.260
we have a global
Layer 7 load balancer

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:23.870
that helps support that.

00:32:23.870 --> 00:32:26.430
That load balancer doesn't
support WebSockets.

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:28.500
So it's like, what do I do?

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:31.440
So WebSockets
themselves on HTTP--

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:33.310
WebSockets run over
a TCP protocol.

00:32:33.310 --> 00:32:36.030
They're negotiated
and kind of managed

00:32:36.030 --> 00:32:38.970
by HTTP, by the browser and
the servers, to, like, how--

00:32:38.970 --> 00:32:40.380
where do I connect to and where?

00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:42.300
But they are a TCP connection.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:45.360
So we need a TCP load balancer--
is essentially what we need.

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:47.580
And Google Cloud Platform
has a TCP load balancer,

00:32:47.580 --> 00:32:48.840
which is great.

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:51.030
But what we also want
is we want SSL support

00:32:51.030 --> 00:32:55.020
because much like HTTP
and HTTPS-- security good.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:58.500
So what we can do, then, is
we set up a TCP load balancer

00:32:58.500 --> 00:32:59.640
and network load balancer.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:03.240
But we set it up with
what's called an SSL proxy.

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:05.640
And once we do that,
things pretty much

00:33:05.640 --> 00:33:07.450
work as you would expect.

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:11.070
So when you go to set up
your network load balancer--

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:14.010
and actually, the
console server--

00:33:14.010 --> 00:33:16.940
console.cloud.google.com
has a really nice interface

00:33:16.940 --> 00:33:19.440
for this now where, if you go
in there and you're like, hey,

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:21.256
I need load balancing
for my network,

00:33:21.256 --> 00:33:22.380
it'll give you the options.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:24.559
You can then select
TCP load balancing.

00:33:24.559 --> 00:33:26.100
And then when you
go in to configure,

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:28.099
it'll actually ask you,
connection termination--

00:33:28.099 --> 00:33:30.810
do you want an SSL proxy or
do you just want straight TCP?

00:33:30.810 --> 00:33:32.685
So you are then able to
select proxy and then

00:33:32.685 --> 00:33:33.850
go configure from there.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:34.190
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:33:34.190 --> 00:33:36.480
MARK MANDEL: So you're
totally able to do WebSockets.

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:38.220
You just need a slightly
different type of load balancer

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:39.390
to run them over.

00:33:39.390 --> 00:33:41.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Little
side question about it--

00:33:41.390 --> 00:33:42.280
you mentioned that--

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:46.787
it's like when it's an SSL load
balancer, you can choose TCP.

00:33:46.787 --> 00:33:48.370
Is there anything
else you can choose?

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:49.660
Can you choose UDP?

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:50.410
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:33:50.410 --> 00:33:51.929
You can totally do that as well.

00:33:51.929 --> 00:33:53.470
It actually has that
as three options

00:33:53.470 --> 00:33:55.282
when you go in,
like HTTP or HTTPS

00:33:55.282 --> 00:33:56.740
load balancing,
TCP load balancing,

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:58.729
as well as UDP load
balancing as well.

00:33:58.729 --> 00:33:59.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:00.680
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

